{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfapgMhzzNR2guCFpR9xv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe behaviour change is wired coherently and the single-source-of-truth for the household rule stays in `OwnerHousehold`, the Javadocs are updated honestly, and `OwnerIdentity` was correctly narrowed to \"descriptive key only\" with the now-dead `forRequest` removed. But the renaming didn't follow the logic move, leaving two stale names:\n\n**1. `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` / `OwnerIdentityDuplicateException` are now misnamed.**\nDuplicate detection no longer keys off the \"identity key\" at all — the class computes `OwnerHousehold.householdId(...)` and rejects a *household* duplicate. Its own new Javadoc says so: \"Rejects a create request that would be a second owner in an existing household.\" Yet:\n- the class is still `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` (it checks household uniqueness, not identity), and\n- it throws `OwnerIdentityDuplicateException`, now constructed with a `householdId` argument (`new OwnerIdentityDuplicateException(householdId)`) — an exception whose name says \"identity\" carrying a household id.\n\nA maintainer reading this pipeline would expect `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` / `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateException`. The \"Identity\" naming was meaningful only while `OwnerIdentity.forRequest` drove the check; that coupling is gone (the class no longer references `OwnerIdentity`), so the names now actively mislead. Rename the class and exception (and the escalation YAML/handler keyed on the FQN) to reflect that this is household-duplicate detection.\n\n**2. `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` recomputes a value existing owners already carry.**\nExisting owners are stamped with `householdId` by `AssignOwnerHousehold` at creation, so the loop could compare against `existing.getHouseholdId()` directly instead of recomputing `OwnerHousehold.householdId(existing.getLastName(), existing.getPostcode())` for every row. Recomputing a persisted field is a small structural smell and quietly re-derives the household rule on the read side; reading the stored id is the more natural expression (and keeps the derivation on the write path only).\n\nMinor / optional: the `Boolean.TRUE.equals(request.getSharesHousehold())` guard now appears in both `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` and `FlagOwnerPossibleDuplicate`. These are genuinely distinct decisions (bypass the block vs. skip the soft-flag), so the duplication is defensible — I'd leave it, but it's worth being aware the \"declared member\" concept is now expressed in two places."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16024,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16024},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb9d70af-0a49-4de5-bc48-d7b166d8d951","uuid":"ba0a5719-0f60-4f5c-807c-29e7d3cd5630","timestamp":"2026-10-01T03:40:58.621Z","request_id":"req_011CfapgMLB5PTUqRYFKVFes"}
